{
  "transactions": [
    {
      "version": "typed-counting-v1",
      "reason": "Contour and surface-mark checks each alternate between two passes and three fails, with conflicting interpretations of softened edges and fine texture. Tonal-modeling checks consistently identify photographic gradients and highlights beneath added texture. Revise the three bindings to require independent, region-specific visual judgments without weakening their conditions, adding duplicate requirements, or prescribing outcomes. Smooth graphite shading remains permissible; smoothness alone is not negative evidence.",
      "actions": [
        {
          "operation": "revise",
          "target_id": "d1_contours",
          "decisions": [
            {
              "id": "d1_contours",
              "requirement_id": "R1",
              "question": "Are the scene's principal shapes and meaningful internal boundaries represented with convincing pencil-like contour linework?",
              "criteria": "Pass if pencil-like contour strokes broadly describe the principal forms and meaningful internal boundaries across the scene. Evidence may include fine irregular graphite-like lines, sketch-like stroke articulation or variable line weight. Contours may follow the source image's boundaries: preserving their geometry does not disqualify a pencil-drawn rendering. Every boundary need not be outlined, and some soft edges may remain. Fail if the principal boundaries still appear photographic or merely sharpened, without broadly convincing drawn linework. Reduced saturation, uniform grain and stronger photographic edges alone do not satisfy this condition. Assess contour appearance independently of interior shading. Use unknown only when visual evidence is insufficient to establish the contour style.",
              "binding": "Compare the supplied SOURCE and EDITED images against the requested pencil-drawing transformation. Independently judge this contour condition as pass, fail or unknown. Inspect the plate rim, potato-slice perimeters and internal marks, carrot boundaries, fork tines, and glass and pitcher outlines. Ground the judgment in the visible character and distribution of boundary strokes: identify where irregular graphite-like articulation, sketch strokes or variable line weight represent forms, and distinguish those features from photographic edge contrast, softened edges or sharpening halos. Use corresponding source boundaries to distinguish drawn representation from retained photographic edges; unchanged geometry is not evidence against pencil contours. Neither desaturation and grain nor incomplete interior shading determines this judgment. Cite concrete boundaries supporting the assessment rather than inferring linework from the overall stylized appearance. Use unknown only if the visual evidence cannot establish contour character or extent, not merely because confidence is low or assessments disagree. The target is pencil-drawn representation of the image's visual content; the reference is the supplied image and the requested pencil-drawing transformation."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "operation": "revise",
          "target_id": "d1_shading_marks",
          "decisions": [
            {
              "id": "d1_shading_marks",
              "requirement_id": "R1",
              "question": "Are the scene's major surfaces represented with pencil-like surface marks or graphite texture?",
              "criteria": "Pass if pencil-like surface mark-making is visibly established across major surfaces through fine directional strokes, hatching, form-following marks or convincing deposited graphite texture. Inspect the marks themselves: they must read as drawing material on the depicted surfaces, rather than merely random noise superimposed on photographic texture. Fine strokes and retained color do not disqualify pencil drawing. Fail if substantial surfaces show only photographic material texture, desaturation or undifferentiated grain without convincing pencil-like marks. This condition concerns surface mark-making, not whether the underlying tonal gradients and reflections have also been converted to pencil shading. Use unknown only when the visible evidence cannot establish the character or extent of the marks.",
              "binding": "Compare the supplied SOURCE and EDITED images against the instruction to change the image into pencil drawing. Independently judge this surface-mark condition as pass, fail or unknown. Inspect the carrots, sprouts, potato slices, plate, table and glassware, comparing corresponding source surfaces to distinguish newly rendered drawing marks from original material texture. Identify concrete regions showing directional strokes, hatching, form-following articulation or convincing deposited graphite texture, and assess whether such marks are established across major surfaces. Distinguish drawing-material texture from undifferentiated speckling over photographic surfaces; describing the image as grainy or textured alone does not establish pencil mark-making. Directional hatching is not mandatory when deposited graphite texture is visibly convincing. Assess the marks themselves, not contour strength, color reduction or completion of underlying tonal modeling. Cite visible examples supporting the judgment. Use unknown only if the evidence cannot establish the marks' character or extent, not merely because confidence is low or assessments disagree. The target is pencil-drawn representation of the image's visual content; the reference is the supplied image and the requested pencil-drawing transformation."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "operation": "revise",
          "target_id": "d1_shading_modeling",
          "decisions": [
            {
              "id": "d1_shading_modeling",
              "requirement_id": "R1",
              "question": "Are the scene's tonal modeling and reflections represented as pencil-drawn shading rather than retained photographic rendering?",
              "criteria": "Pass if pencil-rendered tonal values broadly convey the scene's volume, lighting and reflective surfaces through stroke density, hatching, form-following graphite marks or convincing rubbed graphite tones. Smooth shading can satisfy this condition when it visibly reads as graphite rendering. Isolated imperfect surfaces do not require failure. Fail if substantial surfaces retain photographic gradients, realistic specular reflections or photographic tonal modeling underneath added grain or strokes. Pencil-like surface marks, stronger outlines and desaturation alone do not satisfy this tonal-modeling condition. Assess tonal representation independently of contour linework and the mere presence of surface marks. Use unknown only when visual evidence is insufficient to establish the tonal-rendering style.",
              "binding": "Compare the supplied SOURCE and EDITED images against the instruction to change the image into pencil drawing. Independently judge this tonal-rendering condition as pass, fail or unknown. Inspect the pitcher and wine glass gradients and highlights, the volume modeling of sprouts and carrots, and shaded food, plate and table surfaces. Determine whether the tonal transitions and reflections themselves broadly read as graphite-rendered values or remain photographic beneath added texture. Cite concrete regions and the visible shading characteristics supporting that distinction. Stroke-density modeling, hatching, form-following marks and convincing rubbed graphite tones can establish pencil shading; smooth transitions and preserved lighting geometry alone cannot establish retained photography, since smooth graphite rendering is allowed. Conversely, surface speckling, desaturation and contour changes alone cannot establish converted tonal modeling. Assess the breadth of pencil-rendered modeling across the scene without requiring every highlight or isolated surface to be perfect. Use unknown only when the visual evidence cannot establish tonal-rendering style, not merely because confidence is low or assessments disagree. The target is pencil-drawn representation of the image's visual content; the reference is the supplied image and the requested pencil-drawing transformation."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    }
  ]
}
